WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.509
Minimal API parameter binding converts
request data into handler parameters.

2
00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:16.479
That data may come from route values,
query strings, headers, request bodies,

3
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:20.265
forms, services, or custom binders.

4
00:00:20.345 --> 00:00:27.637
Binding is convenient because it lets the
handler receive typed values.

5
00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:31.662
It is not a security decision by itself.

6
00:00:31.742 --> 00:00:34.850
Bound data remains untrusted.

7
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:42.461
A value that arrives as an integer, GUID,
enum, string, DTO, uploaded file,

8
00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:49.714
or service-resolved object still needs
validation for the operation.

9
00:00:49.794 --> 00:00:55.891
The binder can help create a value in the
expected .NET shape,

10
00:00:55.971 --> 00:01:02.187
but it does not know whether the value is
allowed, meaningful,

11
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:07.049
owned by the caller, or safe for
downstream use.

12
00:01:07.129 --> 00:01:11.791
DTOs help define the intended input
contract.

13
00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:18.685
A create request, update request, search
request, import request,

14
00:01:18.765 --> 00:01:27.133
and administrative request should accept
only the fields the caller is supposed to

15
00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:28.169
provide.

16
00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:36.139
A narrow DTO is easier to validate and
easier to review than a broad domain model

17
00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.490
with many properties.

18
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:46.819
Avoid binding directly to domain models
when doing so would allow over-posting or

19
00:01:46.899 --> 00:01:51.680
accidental modification of sensitive
fields.

20
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:59.531
Server-controlled values such as
ownership, tenant, role, approval state,

21
00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:02.838
pricing, status, audit fields,

22
00:02:02.918 --> 00:02:09.852
or administrative flags should usually be
assigned by server logic,

23
00:02:09.932 --> 00:02:14.116
not accepted from a general request body.

24
00:02:14.196 --> 00:02:17.782
Explicit output models matter too.

25
00:02:17.862 --> 00:02:25.991
The object used internally by the
application may include fields that should not

26
00:02:26.071 --> 00:02:27.864
leave the server.

27
00:02:27.944 --> 00:02:35.595
Response DTOs help avoid accidental
exposure of internal fields, secrets,

28
00:02:35.675 --> 00:02:42.489
flags, tokens, administrative notes, or
implementation details.

29
00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:48.427
Input and output boundaries are both part
of API security.

30
00:02:48.507 --> 00:02:52.691
Request body size, file upload behavior,

31
00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:58.867
and form binding risk should also be
reviewed conceptually.

32
00:02:58.947 --> 00:03:03.251
Large bodies, file data, multipart forms,

33
00:03:03.331 --> 00:03:12.057
and custom binders can consume resources
or introduce parsing assumptions before the

34
00:03:12.137 --> 00:03:16.202
handler performs business validation.

35
00:03:16.282 --> 00:03:22.020
Endpoint design should make those
boundaries explicit.
